A null pointer exception in Java occurs when you try to access or manipulate an object that has been declared as `null`. This can happen if the object is not initialized before being used, or if it's still being created at runtime. 

For example:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null; // declare string variable as null
        System.out.println(str);  // prints "null"
    }
}
```

In this case, attempting to print the value of `str` will result in a `NullPointerException`, because there is no `str` object to print.

To avoid this issue, you should always check for `null` before trying to use any object:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null; // declare string variable as null
        if (str != null) {
            System.out.println(str); // prints "null"
        } else {
            System.out.println("String is null");
        }
    }
}
```

